---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import RelatedArticles from '../components/shared/RelatedArticles.astro';
import MidContentAd from '../components/shared/MidContentAd.astro';
import EndOfContentAd from '../components/shared/EndOfContentAd.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/shared/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import EditorialStandards from '../components/shared/EditorialStandards.astro';

const SITE = 'https://vitamindcalculator.net';
const PAGE_URL = `${SITE}/vitamin-d-and-anxiety`;

const medicalJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'MedicalWebPage',
  name: 'Vitamin D and Anxiety',
  url: PAGE_URL,
  medicalAudience: { '@type': 'MedicalAudience', audienceType: 'Patient' },
  about: [{ '@type': 'MedicalCondition', name: 'Anxiety disorder', code: { '@type': 'MedicalCode', codeValue: 'F41', codingSystem: 'ICD-10' } }],
  lastReviewed: '2026-08-14',
};
const breadcrumbJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
  itemListElement: [
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: SITE },
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Vitamin D and Anxiety', item: PAGE_URL },
  ],
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Vitamin D and Anxiety: What the Evidence Actually Supports"
  description="The vitamin D–anxiety link is thinner than the depression evidence. What observational studies and the few trials show, and why correcting a deficiency is worth doing but rarely a cure."
  ogType="article"
>
  <script slot="head" type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(medicalJsonLd)} />
  <script slot="head" type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(breadcrumbJsonLd)} />

  <Breadcrumbs crumbs={[{ href: "/vitamin-d-and-anxiety", label: "Vitamin D & Anxiety" }]} />
  <EditorialStandards />

  <main class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
    <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">Vitamin D and Anxiety</h1>
    <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-8">
      Vitamin D receptors are found throughout the brain, including regions that regulate mood and
      fear, so it is reasonable to ask whether low vitamin D contributes to anxiety. The honest answer
      is that the evidence for anxiety is <em>weaker</em> than for
      <a href="/vitamin-d-and-mental-health" class="text-amber-700 underline">depression</a>, and much
      of it is tangled up with the effects of light, season, and lifestyle rather than the vitamin
      itself.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">What the observational data show</h2>
    <MidContentAd />
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      Cross-sectional studies do find that people with lower 25(OH)D report more anxiety symptoms on
      average, and anxiety is more common in winter and at high latitudes. But this is exactly the
      setting where confounding is hardest to escape: people who are anxious may go outside less, be
      less active, sleep worse, and eat differently — all of which lower vitamin D. Reduced daylight
      itself affects mood through circadian and serotonergic pathways independent of vitamin D. So a
      correlation between low vitamin D and anxiety does not tell us the vitamin is the cause.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">The trial evidence</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      Randomised trials with anxiety as a primary outcome are few and small. Some studies in specific
      groups — for example women with type 2 diabetes and anxiety symptoms — have reported modest
      improvements with supplementation, but these are small, short, and not consistently replicated.
      Large mood-focused trials and the mental-health arms of big vitamin D studies have generally not
      shown that supplementing an already-sufficient population reduces anxiety. The pattern, as with
      depression, is that any benefit is most plausible in people who start out genuinely deficient.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">A reasonable, honest approach</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed space-y-2">
      <li><strong>Check the level if you have symptoms and risk factors</strong> — deficiency is common and easy to correct.</li>
      <li><strong>Correct a genuine deficiency</strong> — low-risk, generally worthwhile, and may help a subset.</li>
      <li><strong>Do not expect it to replace treatment.</strong> The interventions with strong evidence for anxiety are psychological therapy (especially CBT), regular exercise, sleep, and, where indicated, medication.</li>
      <li><strong>Get outdoors.</strong> Daylight, movement, and nature exposure plausibly help mood through several routes at once — vitamin D is only one of them.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Bottom line</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      Fixing a real vitamin D deficiency is sensible and might take the edge off symptoms for some
      people, but the evidence does not support vitamin D as a treatment for anxiety. If anxiety is
      affecting your life, the therapies that actually move the needle deserve first call on your
      attention.
    </p>

    <EndOfContentAd />

    <div class="bg-blue-50 border border-blue-200 rounded-xl p-4 text-sm text-blue-900 mt-8 leading-relaxed">
      <strong>Not medical advice.</strong> If anxiety is persistent or interfering with daily life,
      please speak to a healthcare professional. Effective treatments exist, and vitamin D is at best a
      minor supporting player.
    </div>

    <RelatedArticles current="/vitamin-d-and-anxiety" />
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
